{
  "case_id": "2020-19-108",
  "document_hash": "3bebd06536902936b95d03e16f95fe8c7c9aafb0aad869347b1110bb087845db",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2020-19-108",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "3bebd06536902936b95d03e16f95fe8c7c9aafb0aad869347b1110bb087845db",
      "evidence_id": "ev-3bebd0653690-0000-0000",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 2,
        "start": 2
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The above-entitled matter came on for oral argument before the Supreme Court of the United States at 10:00 a.m."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "General Wall",
      "case_id": "2020-19-108",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "3bebd06536902936b95d03e16f95fe8c7c9aafb0aad869347b1110bb087845db",
      "evidence_id": "ev-3bebd0653690-0000-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 5,
        "start": 5
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "GENERAL WALL: Mr. Chief Justice, and may it please the Court: When Congress said in Article 43 of the UCMJ that an offense punishable by death may be prosecuted at any time, Congress was referring to the punishment it had provided for in the very same code."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "General Wall",
      "case_id": "2020-19-108",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "3bebd06536902936b95d03e16f95fe8c7c9aafb0aad869347b1110bb087845db",
      "evidence_id": "ev-3bebd0653690-0000-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 6,
        "start": 6
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "provisions mirror each other. Article 120 says rape may be punished by death, and Article 43 then refers to offenses punishable by death. Congress naturally was referring in the limitations provision to the punishment it had fixed in the penalty provision."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2020-19-108",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "3bebd06536902936b95d03e16f95fe8c7c9aafb0aad869347b1110bb087845db",
      "evidence_id": "ev-3bebd0653690-0000-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 7,
        "start": 7
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "CHIEF JUSTICE ROBERTS: General, you've been talking about what Congress did, but, of course, the -- the issue comes from what the Court did."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Alito",
      "case_id": "2020-19-108",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "3bebd06536902936b95d03e16f95fe8c7c9aafb0aad869347b1110bb087845db",
      "evidence_id": "ev-3bebd0653690-0000-0005",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 17,
        "start": 17
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE ALITO: There are some canons of interpretation that may work against you in this situation, such as the principle that statutes of limitation are to be narrowly interpreted and the Rule of Lenity. Could you explain why you think the statutory language is clear enough to overcome those?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "General Wall",
      "case_id": "2020-19-108",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "3bebd06536902936b95d03e16f95fe8c7c9aafb0aad869347b1110bb087845db",
      "evidence_id": "ev-3bebd0653690-0001-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 27,
        "start": 27
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "That isn't normally the way we read limitations provisions, and as I tried to say at the top of the hour, there's a lot of textual, contextual, and historical evidence here that that's not what Congress was doing in Article 43."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Stephen I. Vladeck",
      "case_id": "2020-19-108",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "3bebd06536902936b95d03e16f95fe8c7c9aafb0aad869347b1110bb087845db",
      "evidence_id": "ev-3bebd0653690-0001-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 35,
        "start": 35
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Since 1953, every court to consider it, including CAAF, has correctly understood Article 55 of the UCMJ to reflect Congress's specific judgment that service members should receive the same protection against cruel and unusual punishment that civilians enjoy under the Eighth Amendment."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Breyer",
      "case_id": "2020-19-108",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "3bebd06536902936b95d03e16f95fe8c7c9aafb0aad869347b1110bb087845db",
      "evidence_id": "ev-3bebd0653690-0002-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 44,
        "start": 44
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE BREYER: Did -- did -- did -- did you -- did you -- I know that this may be only a very few people, perhaps me, who find this sometimes helpful, but did you, during all this period, find anything in legislative history or in presentation by the military to Congress or in articles in the military that said this is a good idea to have the statute of limitations work this way, to cut it down, in effect, the law?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Vladeck",
      "case_id": "2020-19-108",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "3bebd06536902936b95d03e16f95fe8c7c9aafb0aad869347b1110bb087845db",
      "evidence_id": "ev-3bebd0653690-0002-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 44,
        "start": 44
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. VLADECK: No, Justice Breyer, and I think that only reinforces our position that Congress was not thinking specifically about rape at all in 1986 and that when it finally is thinking specifically about rape, as it clearly was in 2003 and 2006, both of those statutes proceeded from the assumption that the law on the books was that the statute of limitations Heritage Reporting Corporation"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Alito",
      "case_id": "2020-19-108",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "3bebd06536902936b95d03e16f95fe8c7c9aafb0aad869347b1110bb087845db",
      "evidence_id": "ev-3bebd0653690-0002-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 46,
        "start": 46
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE ALITO: Suppose someone in the military commits a murder, but this individual has diminished mental capacity so that it would be unconstitutional under Atkins and related cases to impose the death penalty. Would that crime be an offense that is \"punishable by death\"?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Vladeck",
      "case_id": "2020-19-108",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "3bebd06536902936b95d03e16f95fe8c7c9aafb0aad869347b1110bb087845db",
      "evidence_id": "ev-3bebd0653690-0002-0008",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 46,
        "start": 46
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "uncertain",
      "text": "And you don't have to take my word for that, Justice Breyer. In the Moore case in 1990, the Navy-Marine Corps Court of Military Review observed that the 1986 amendment changed the statute of limitations from three years to five years. And the Air Force Court reached the exact same conclusion three years later in the Bottino case."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Vladeck",
      "case_id": "2020-19-108",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "3bebd06536902936b95d03e16f95fe8c7c9aafb0aad869347b1110bb087845db",
      "evidence_id": "ev-3bebd0653690-0002-0003",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 48,
        "start": 48
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Congress chose the words, Justice Alito, \"offense punishable by death.\" It's not a separate offense when you charge capital murder against a juvenile or a --a diminished-capacity offender. It's just a separate charge based on their capacity."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Vladeck",
      "case_id": "2020-19-108",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "3bebd06536902936b95d03e16f95fe8c7c9aafb0aad869347b1110bb087845db",
      "evidence_id": "ev-3bebd0653690-0002-0007",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 52,
        "start": 52
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. VLADECK: So we have two responses, Justice Kagan. The first is, even on that reading, the code alone includes Article 55. And so, even if we were to read the text to add the words the government would have us add, we end up in the same place because Article 55, as it has been uniformly interpreted for 70 years, applies Coker to courts-martial."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Gorsuch",
      "case_id": "2020-19-108",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "3bebd06536902936b95d03e16f95fe8c7c9aafb0aad869347b1110bb087845db",
      "evidence_id": "ev-3bebd0653690-0002-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 54,
        "start": 54
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GORSUCH: Good morning, Mr. Vladeck. As I understand it -- and you can -- please do correct me if I'm mistaken -- both sides agree that when we're interpreting the term \"punishable by death,\" it -- we -- it -- nothing turns on and -- and -- and we shouldn't look to facts outside of the -- the -- the indictment or the complaint or the statute."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Respondents",
      "case_id": "2020-19-108",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "3bebd06536902936b95d03e16f95fe8c7c9aafb0aad869347b1110bb087845db",
      "evidence_id": "ev-3bebd0653690-0003-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 64,
        "start": 64
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Respondents' so-called plain meaning argument, I didn't hear Respondents' counsel answer -- give any answer to the parallelism in the language between Articles 43 and 120 or the express language of Article 18 that points to whether death is authorized by the code, and that's the UCMJ."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Thomas",
      "case_id": "2020-19-108",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "3bebd06536902936b95d03e16f95fe8c7c9aafb0aad869347b1110bb087845db",
      "evidence_id": "ev-3bebd0653690-0003-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 65,
        "start": 65
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "when you -- when Justice Thomas pressed Respondents' counsel on the meaning of Article 55, you know, he turned to the military courts, not text and history."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Respondents",
      "case_id": "2020-19-108",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "3bebd06536902936b95d03e16f95fe8c7c9aafb0aad869347b1110bb087845db",
      "evidence_id": "ev-3bebd0653690-0003-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 66,
        "start": 66
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "correctly acknowledged to you that to prevail, he needs the Court to endorse the proposition that the death penalty is never a constitutionally permissible punishment for -- for rape inside the military."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2020-19-108",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "3bebd06536902936b95d03e16f95fe8c7c9aafb0aad869347b1110bb087845db",
      "evidence_id": "ev-3bebd0653690-0003-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 67,
        "start": 67
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "CHIEF JUSTICE ROBERTS: Thank you, General, Mr. Vladeck. The case is submitted."
    },
    {
